# Testable Independent Phases

When planning and building extension actions, structure work into independent, testable phases.

## Planning

- Each step in the implementation plan must be a self-contained phase that can be verified in isolation before moving to the next.
- A phase should have clearly defined inputs, outputs, and success criteria so tests can assert on it independently.
- Do not bundle unrelated concerns (e.g. auth extraction + DOM manipulation + API call) into a single phase. Split them so each can be tested on its own.
- Present the phase breakdown to the user for approval before implementation begins.

## Testing Requirements

- Every phase must include both unit tests and integration tests before it is considered complete.
- Unit tests validate the phase's logic in isolation (mocked dependencies, edge cases, error paths).
- Integration tests validate the phase works correctly with its real dependencies (API helpers, shared utilities, other phases).
- Tests must pass before moving to the next phase. Do not defer test writing to the end.

## Phase Independence

- Each phase must be independently runnable and testable without requiring later phases to exist.
- Shared utilities and API helpers introduced in one phase must be tested in that same phase, not deferred.
- If a phase depends on output from a prior phase, mock that output in unit tests and use the real prior phase in integration tests.
- When a phase is complete and tested, it should be safe to merge or review on its own.
